In aluminium castings, strength is mainly achieved through solid solution strengthening mechanism which involves the incorporation of alloying elements in the matrix. In aluminium foundry alloys, solid solution strengthening is mainly achieved by Mg and Si. Mg has a high solubility in the aluminium matrix, so when incorporated, it significantly enhances the yield strength through lattice distortion. Moreover, Mg atoms, being larger than aluminium atoms cause substantial strain in the lattice and form the Cottrell atmosphere 29  below the dislocation line, hindering dislocation motion and hence providing a pronounced strengthening effect. On the other hand, Si, due to its limited solubility in aluminium matrix, provides slight solid solution strengthening but mainly contributes to an increase in castability and wear resistance. While looking for their alternative alloying elements, Copper (Cu), Zinc (Zn), and Manganese (Mn) provide reliable alternative options for solid solution strengthening and their solubility increases as temperature increases. All these elements have significant solid solubility in the aluminium matrix. Cu atoms like Mg replace aluminium atoms in the crystal lattice, causing lattice distortions, that represent an obstacle to the dislocation motion. Concerning Zn, it has a smaller atomic radius compared to Al, and contributes to solid solution strengthening, however, its effect in comparison with Mg is way less. Similarly, Mn, which primarily aids in controlling grain structure, can also dissolve in aluminium matrix to some extent to contribute for solid solution strengthening. The overall yield strength is assumed to be enhanced by the addition of solute atoms and each contribution can be calculated through the well-known equations 30 , 31 1 σ ss = ∑ j k j C i j 2 / 3 \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
